   a. Poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM): This is a thermosicritical polymer that undergoes a phase transition at a temperature of 40°C. At temperatures below this, it is in a swollen, hydrated state, while above this, it collapses into a hydrophobic state.
   b. Poly(ethylene methacrylate) (PPEMA): This polymer has a lower critical temperature (LCT) around 5.5°C, where it transitions from a swollen to a glassy state.
   c. Stimellarized polymers: These polymers have thermoicritical behavior, which can be tuned by changing the molecular weight, cross-linking density, or the presence of additives.

2. pH-sensitive polymers: These polymers respond to changes in pH by undergoing changes in their charge density, which can lead to changes in their solubility or mechanical properties. Examples include:
   a. Poly(acrylic acid) (PAA) and poly(acrylic acid-acrylic acid-acrylate) (PAA-AA-AA-AA): These polymers are hydrophilic at high pH and hydrophobic at low pH due to the presence of carboxylic acid groups.
   b. Poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA) and poly(vinyl alcohol-methacrylate) (PVA-MA): These polymers are hydrophilic at high pH and hydrophobic at low pH due to the presence of hydroxyl groups.

3. Light-sensitive polymers: These polymers undergo changes in their properties upon exposure to light, such as through photodegradation, crosslinking, or aggregation. Examples include:
   a. Poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM) grafted with light-responsive groups: These polymers can undergo changes in their properties upon exposure to light, such as swelling or shrinking.
   b. Polymers with photo-crosslinking agents: These polymers can undergo crosslinking upon exposure to light, leading to changes in their mechanical properties.

