   \[
   \lambda = \frac{h}{2\pi m_0 k_B \sqrt{T}}
   \]
   where:
   - \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\) (Planck's constant)
   - \(m_0 = 4 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) (mass of a helium atom)
   - \(k_B = 1.380 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J/K}\) (Boltzmann constant)
   - \(T = 290 \, \text{K}\) (temperature)

   Substituting the values:
   \[
   \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{2\pi \times 4 \times 10^{-27} \times 1.380 \times 10^{-23} \times \sqrt{290}} \, \text{m}
   \]
   \[
   \lambda = 1.6 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m}
   \]

2. **Free Helmholtz Factor (\(\exp(\mu/kT)\))**:
   For an ideal gas, the meanolecular Gibbs free energy (\(\mu\)) is:
   \[
   \mu = -kT \ln(p)
   \]
   Thus, the free Helmholtz factor is:
   \[
   \exp(\mu/kT) = \exp\left(\frac{-kT \ln(p)}{kT}\right) = \exp\left(-\ln(p)\right) = \frac{1}{p}
   \]
   Given \(p = 1 \, \text{bar} = 10^5 \, \text{Pa}\):
   \[
   \exp(\mu/kT) = \frac{1}{10^5} = 10^{-5}
   \]

