The uncertainty principle is given by:
\[
\Delta E \cdot \tau = \hbar /2
\]
where \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(\hbar = frac{h}{2\pi} \approx 1.00 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}\)).

First, we need to convert the lifetime from seconds to meters per second (since the energy is given in MeV, we will need to convert the energy to joules for consistency with \(\hbar\)):
\[
\tau = 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}
\]

Now, we can solve for \(\Delta E\):
\[
\Delta E = \frac{\hbar}{2\tau} = \frac{1.00 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]

Convert \(\Delta E\) from joules to MeV:
\[
\Delta E = 5 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1}{931.5 \text{ MeV J}} = 5.36 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the uncertainty in the rest energy is \(\boxed{5.36 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}}\).

Thus, the typical range of outcomes for the measurement of the σ⁺ rest energy is:
\[
5.36 \times 10^{-13} \text{ MeV}
\]

