","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
F = F_{\text{net}} = qE
\]  
where \(F\) is the gravitational force, \(q\) is the charge of the proton, and \(E\) is the electric field strength.  

\[
mg = qE
\]  
The net force on the proton is the electrostatic force, which is \(F = qE\).  

\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) \times (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}
\]  
Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we solve for the acceleration \(a\):  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.78 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The acceleration of the proton is \(\boxed{4.78 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  

(b) The acceleration \(a\) is constant, and the proton starts from rest (\(v = 0\)), so we use the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = v_0 + at
\]  
